<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Why this file exists, so nobody deletes the four PEMs beside it.

    www.mojon.es is served by Let's Encrypt, and the chain it presents today is

        CN=mojon.es  <-  CN=YR1  <-  CN=Root YR, cross-signed by ISRG Root X1

    so the anchor a client must hold is ISRG Root X1, and only devices whose trust store
    was updated after 2016 hold it. The Fire TV Stick 4K runs Fire OS 6 on Android 7.1, and
    Amazon's trust store there is Amazon's, not Google's — it may or may not carry X1, and
    "may" is not something a release can be built on. There is no fallback to check against
    either: the site sends HSTS and redirects http to https, so a failed handshake is a blank
    app rather than a degraded one.

    Both generations are bundled, not just the one in use. Let's Encrypt is migrating to the
    Root YR / Root YE pair and will stop cross-signing them from X1 once enough devices carry
    them — the day it does, an app trusting only X1 stops working on exactly the old hardware
    this exists for. Bundling costs four kilobytes.

    `src="system"` is kept and listed first: everything that already works must keep working,
    and this only adds anchors. The user store is deliberately absent — nothing here needs to
    be interceptable, and a debug-only override belongs in a debug manifest, not in this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_x1" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_x2" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_yr" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_ye" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
